<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The F3 telemetry overlay.

  A full-width sheet rather than the top-right box it used to be. At a 620px minimum in the
  corner it drew straight through the match panel on a 1080-wide portrait screen, which is the
  one screen where a diagnostics overlay most needs to be readable; and the numbers it reports
  are wide rows of tabular figures, which a corner box was always going to wrap.

  The verdict label sits above the graph and is the only part of the sheet written in
  sentences. The rows below it are twenty true numbers with no ranking between them, which is
  the right tool at a desk with a profiler open and the wrong one on a phone at arm's length,
  where the question is only ever "is this build broken, and by what". It is above the graph
  rather than below the readout because it is the line to read first.

  Two tabs, because the overlay now answers two different questions. FRAME is the renderer and
  the allocator - what the performance rules set budgets for. COMBAT is the simulation: how
  often the policy is being asked for a decision, how much is being thrown, what the director
  is looking at. Neither belongs in the other's column, and stacking both would make a sheet
  tall enough to cover the fight it is reporting on. MATCH is the third: the whole match a
  second at a time from PerformanceLogView, with thermal state, for the slowdown a phone only
  shows as it heats.

  The graph element is empty by design: its contents are painted through
  generateVisualContent with Painter2D, so there is nothing here for UXML to declare beyond
  the box it draws into and the class that gives that box its size and ground.

  DEBUG opens the sheet collapsed to the verdict alone, docked above the bottom chrome row: one
  sentence saying whether the build is healthy, which is the whole question at arm's length on a
  phone. Tapping the verdict opens the tabs, the graph and the figures; tapping it again folds
  them away. A developer who wants the numbers is one tap from them, and nobody else has a
  full-width opaque sheet parked over the ring.

  The tabs and the verdict are deliberately NOT picking-mode="Ignore" - they are the only
  things on this sheet anyone clicks. Everything else is, because the overlay sits over a live
  ring and must never eat a click meant for a panel below it.
-->
<ui:UXML xmlns:ui="UnityEngine.UIElements">
    <ui:VisualElement name="safe-outline" class="diag__safe diag__safe--hidden" picking-mode="Ignore" />
    <ui:VisualElement name="panel" class="diag diag--collapsed">
        <ui:VisualElement name="tabs" class="diag__tabs">
            <ui:Label name="title" text="DEBUG   F3" class="diag__title" picking-mode="Ignore" />
            <ui:Button name="tab-frame" text="FRAME" class="diag__tab diag__tab--on" />
            <ui:Button name="tab-combat" text="COMBAT" class="diag__tab" />
            <ui:Button name="tab-match" text="MATCH" class="diag__tab" />
        </ui:VisualElement>

        <ui:Label name="verdict" class="diag__verdict" />
        <ui:VisualElement name="graph" class="diag__graph" picking-mode="Ignore" />
        <ui:Label name="readout" class="diag__readout" picking-mode="Ignore" />
    </ui:VisualElement>
</ui:UXML>
